WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:05.400
The Last Jedi received plenty of justifiable
criticism, but regardless of what people thought

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:11.620
of the movie as a whole, I saw a lot of people
talking about how cool this particular scene

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:13.389
is.

00:00:13.389 --> 00:00:18.660
In a franchise where large explosions in space
has been done many, many times, the fact that

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:25.080
director Ryan Johnson was able to create an
explosion scene that felt fresh and was breathtaking

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:26.080
is impressive.

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:28.060
So what is it about this scene that makes
it so cool?

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:33.880
Let’s breakdown all the elements and see
what we find.

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:45.830
First let’s talk about the context of the
scene in the story.

00:00:45.830 --> 00:00:49.670
The build up to this moment involves several
scenes that are being intercut.

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:53.980
Leading up to the explosion three separate
stories are reaching a crescendo.

00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:59.100
This has the effect of making this explosion
feel like the fulfillment of more pieces of

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:02.040
the story than it actually is.

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:04.783
One of the pretty obvious effects used to
create the forcefulness of the impact of this

00:01:04.783 --> 00:01:07.970
scene is the way the audio drops out entirely.

00:01:07.970 --> 00:01:12.460
Surrounded on either side by music, dialogue
and sound effects, the suddenness of the silence

00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:16.970
in this moment creates a heavy impact for
the audience.

00:01:16.970 --> 00:01:22.230
But the most brilliant piece of why this explosion
looks so cool is also one of the most subtle

00:01:22.230 --> 00:01:23.230
parts.

00:01:23.230 --> 00:01:26.050
To understand what’s going on, we need to
talk about a technical part of cameras for

00:01:26.050 --> 00:01:27.050
a moment.

00:01:27.050 --> 00:01:29.600
Working with a real explosion and physical
cameras, a burst of light, like the kind that

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:32.430
comes from an explosion can present a challenge.

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:37.540
The light from the explosion is much brighter
than the ambient light of a nighttime scene

00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:42.850
or sky, so to compensate for this a cinematographer
would have a few options.

00:01:42.850 --> 00:01:46.530
The first would be to set the exposure like
they normally would for the scene, and just

00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:48.950
let the bright fireball be overexposed.

00:01:48.950 --> 00:01:52.860
JJ Abrams uses this method in The Force Awakens,
in these scenes.

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:58.150
Here’s a real life example, in this footage
of a nuclear explosion, the camera’s exposure

00:01:58.150 --> 00:02:00.260
is set for a daylight scene.

00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:04.580
Because the explosion is so much brighter,
the image is completely blown out for a few

00:02:04.580 --> 00:02:05.580
seconds.

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:10.030
The second would be to set the exposure for
the brightness of the explosion and let everything

00:02:10.030 --> 00:02:13.390
else in the scene be darker or in silhouette
as a result.

00:02:13.390 --> 00:02:16.659
Abrams also uses this effect in this scene
from The Force Awakens.

00:02:16.659 --> 00:02:17.659
Here’s a another real life example, because
his explosion is so much brighter than the

00:02:17.659 --> 00:02:18.659
surrounding, when the camera is set to capture
the explosion, everything else is in complete

00:02:18.659 --> 00:02:19.659
darkness.

00:02:19.659 --> 00:02:22.189
The third option would be to ramp or change
the exposure as the explosion is happening.

00:02:22.189 --> 00:02:27.540
A camera with auto exposure controls does
this, you can see it in action by pointing

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:30.019
your phone at a light bulb and then turning
it on.

00:02:30.019 --> 00:02:33.620
This is the effect Rian Johnson uses in this
scene.

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:37.390
When you’re dealing with virtual camera
and digital effects, like Rian Johnson would

00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:42.359
have been with this scene, it’s technically
possible to to artificially adjust the brightness

00:02:42.359 --> 00:02:47.599
of the explosion so you don’t have the exposure
“problem” you would in real life.

00:02:47.599 --> 00:02:52.000
This is done quite often in the Original Star
Wars trilogy and the prequels.

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:56.290
In this shot, if the camera were to expose
for the brightness of the explosion, we shouldn’t

00:02:56.290 --> 00:03:01.159
be able to see the stars in the background
at all, which are comparatively dim.

00:03:01.159 --> 00:03:02.639
This can look artificial.

00:03:02.639 --> 00:03:08.049
Instead Johnson chooses to have the virtual
camera adjust to the brightness of the explosion

00:03:08.049 --> 00:03:13.239
as it happens, causing a brief moment of overexposure
before the camera adjusts to the brightness

00:03:13.239 --> 00:03:16.829
of the explosion, leaving everything else
in the scene in darkness.

00:03:16.829 --> 00:03:21.770
Notice how the stars which can be seen before
the explosion, can’t be seen after the camera’s

00:03:21.770 --> 00:03:23.109
exposure adjusts.

00:03:23.109 --> 00:03:28.739
I think this subtle detail lends a realistic
quality to the sequence of shots.

00:03:28.739 --> 00:03:33.549
Making the explosion feel grounded within
a physical reality and emphasizing how bright

00:03:33.549 --> 00:03:35.239
the explosion actually is.

00:03:35.239 --> 00:03:40.129
Johnson doesn’t linger on the explosion
to long, he quickly grounds the effect within

00:03:40.129 --> 00:03:45.010
the space the character’s inhabit, which
helps place the explosion in perspective.

00:03:45.010 --> 00:03:50.400
It’s interesting to me that a small detail
like this can be so effective.

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:55.120
Small details can sell a scene, and the strange
part is that they work even for an audience

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:58.860
member who never notices it or doesn’t know
what’s going on.

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:03.560
Even if you have no idea how a camera works,
whenever we use our phone camera, or any kind

00:04:03.560 --> 00:04:06.769
of video camera, we pick up small clues about
how things look.

00:04:06.769 --> 00:04:11.269
If the exposure of the explosion and the scene
had been artificially balanced.

00:04:11.269 --> 00:04:15.670
Something might have looked a little bit off
to the average audience member, but the probably

00:04:15.670 --> 00:04:18.480
wouldn’t have been able to explain why.

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:24.310
Recreating the limitations of a physical camera
with visual effects helps things feel more

00:04:24.310 --> 00:04:25.310
real.

00:04:25.310 --> 00:04:28.220
In filmmaking, details matter.

00:04:28.220 --> 00:04:30.441
Even the small ones that only effect a few
frames.

